          "ruleId": "CWE-918",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Host/authority confusion in parse-url's git-ssh fallback: attacker-controlled `url` reaches `parsed.href.match(GIT_RE)` (src/index.js:67), and `parsed.resource` and `parsed.host` are then assigned from `matched[4]` (src/index.js:71-72) \u2014 capture group 4 of GIT_RE is `([\\w\\.\\-@]+)`, a greedy class that PERMITS the `@` character. For an input that parse-path marks `parse_failed` (e.g. `https://evil.com@good.com/p` or `git@evil.com@good.com/x`), group 4 greedily captures the entire `evil.com@good.com` span, so the returned `host`/`resource` is not the true URL authority. Consumers of parse-url use `parsed.resource`/`parsed.host` to choose a connection target or to make allowlist/host-validation decisions, so the returned authority disagrees with what an HTTP/SSH client would actually contact \u2014 SSRF / access-control bypass. Note the hand-rolled regex OVERRIDES the real parser's output rather than validating it, and `parsed.parse_failed` is reset to false (L75) so callers see the result as a clean parse.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. src/index.js:38 defines GIT_RE whose 4th capture group is ([\\w\\.\\-@]+) \u2014 I counted the groups myself L-to-R (g1 outer, g2 scheme alternation, g3 (s)?, g4 host class, g5 (/|:), g6/g7 path), confirming group 4 is the authority slot and that it permits and greedily consumes '@'. On the parse_failed path (L66) the function then OVERRIDES the real parser: parsed.resource = matched[4] (L71), parsed.host = matched[4] (L72), parsed.user is hardcoded to \"git\" discarding the real userinfo (L73), and parsed.parse_failed is reset to false (L75), so callers see a clean parse. Because GIT_RE is unanchored at the end and its host class allows multiple '@', an input that WHATWG new URL() rejects but GIT_RE matches yields host/resource that is NOT the authority an SSH/HTTP client would contact \u2014 e.g. `git@evil.com@good.com:org/repo.git` gives host=\"evil.com@good.com\" while ssh(1) would connect to good.com; `https://evil.com@good.com:99999/p` (port overflow makes new URL throw) likewise gives host=\"evil.com@good.com\". The branch is NOT dead: the package's own test at test/index.mjs:120-135 expects protocols:[\"ssh\"], user:\"git\", pathname:\"/my-org/my-repo.git\", port:\"\" for `git@github.my-enterprise.com:my-org/my-repo.git` \u2014 values only L69-75 can set, with port:\"\"/password:\"\" being parse-path's failure-branch defaults, proving scp-style URLs reach GIT_RE. Reachability confirmed by my own Cypher: EntryPoint{kind:\"handler\", exposure:\"reachable\"} -> src/index.js::program:parseUrl (L35), and L67/L71/L72/L75 all reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1, with a FLOWS_TO chain normalizeUrl@60 -> parsePath@63 -> parsed.href.match(GIT_RE)@67. Same defect ships in the build artifacts (dist/index.js:46,74-83; dist/index.mjs:39,67-76). Two corrections to the analyst: (a) their PoC `https://evil.com@good.com/p` does NOT reach the branch \u2014 new URL() parses it successfully (username=\"evil.com\", host=\"good.com\") so parse_failed=false; the scp-style and port-overflow variants do. (b) Impact is downstream/consumer-dependent since this library makes no network call itself, so the SSRF/allowlist-bypass is realized in consumers (git-url-parse / git-up and similar) that trust parsed.host/parsed.resource as the connection target or allowlist key. exploit_search was unavailable (\"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"), so no exploit-module/CVE version comparison was performed and none is claimed as evidence; version is pinned as parse-url 8.0.0 at package.json:3."
          },
