        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-436",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Protocol downgrade/confusion: when the GIT_RE fallback matches, src/index.js:69-70 unconditionally hardcodes `parsed.protocols = [\"ssh\"]` and `parsed.protocol = \"ssh\"`, but GIT_RE's leading alternation is `^(git@|http(s)?:\\/\\/)` \u2014 it explicitly ACCEPTS `http://` and `https://` inputs, not just `git@`. So an attacker-supplied `http://`/`https://` URL that parse-path fails on is relabelled as protocol \"ssh\" (and `parsed.user` is forced to \"git\" at L73). Any consumer that branches on `parsed.protocol` or allowlists protocols (a very common parse-url usage) is given a scheme that contradicts the input, enabling scheme-based validation bypass or routing the request through an unintended transport.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source, the lead's structural claim is exactly right. src/index.js:38 defines GIT_RE with the alternation `(git@|http(s)?:\\/\\/)`, which explicitly accepts http:// and https:// inputs. In the `parse_failed` fallback at src/index.js:66-75, once `parsed.href.match(GIT_RE)` is truthy the code unconditionally hardcodes `parsed.protocols=[\"ssh\"]` (L69), `parsed.protocol=\"ssh\"` (L70), forges `parsed.user=\"git\"` (L73), and clears `parsed.parse_failed=false` (L75). Capture groups 2 and 3 - the ones that record WHICH scheme matched - are never read anywhere in the file, so nothing conditions the relabelling on the input actually being an ssh/git URL. The result: an http(s) URL is returned to the caller tagged as protocol \"ssh\", with an injected \"git\" user, its port silently dropped, and parse_failed=false so the forged result is indistinguishable from a clean parse. This is an interpretation conflict (CWE-436) in a library whose sole contract is to report a URL's protocol; consumers that allowlist or branch on `.protocol` (the dominant parse-url usage) get a scheme contradicting the input. I verified this is live shipped code, not dead src: dist/index.js:46,74-87 is logically identical and package.json:5 makes dist/index.js the `main` entry. The fallback branch is genuinely exercised - test/index.mjs:185-189 asserts parseUrl(\"foo\") throws \"URL parsing failed\", reachable only via the parse_failed===true guard at L66, confirming parse-path@7 sets parse_failed for inputs its new URL() rejects. My own graph queries show every call on L69-75 with reachable_from_entry=true and hop_distance=1, and src/index.js::program:parseUrl as an EntryPoint with exposure=reachable, so no reachability downgrade applies. Concrete trigger I derived: `https://evil.com:1234567890/path` - a port above 65535 is a spec-mandated WHATWG URL parse failure, so new URL() throws and parse_failed becomes true, then GIT_RE matches with group2=\"https://\", group4=\"evil.com\", group5=\":\", group7=\"1234567890/path\", yielding protocol \"ssh\" / user \"git\" / host \"evil.com\". Caveats I am explicit about: parse-path@7 is neither vendored nor in the graph (CpgFile lists only example/index.js and src/index.js) and Bash is disabled, so I could not read that dependency or execute the trigger - the \"invalid port => parse_failed\" step rests on the in-repo test plus the URL spec rather than direct observation; the unconditional-relabelling defect itself is confirmed purely by direct source reading and does not depend on it. The mandated exploit_search cross-check returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no version-range comparison could be performed and I do not claim a weaponized exploit; the pinned version is exactly 8.0.0 (package.json:3). I widened the analyst's location from 69-73 to 69-75, since L75's `parse_failed=false` is what lets the forged scheme escape as a successful parse. Severity MEDIUM rather than HIGH because impact is contingent on the downstream consumer's use of .protocol and no sink exists in this repo; it rises to HIGH for consumers that enforce scheme allowlists on that field."
          },
