        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-436",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "The JSDoc contains an explicit acknowledgement that the normalization protection does not cover the ssh/git path \u2014 'For SSH urls, normalize won't work.' (src/index.js:19, inside the parseUrl doc block). That documented gap lines up exactly with the GIT_RE fallback at lines 66-75, which is the branch that fabricates security-relevant output fields. Concretely, GIT_RE at line 38 accepts `http(s)?:\\/\\/` in its first alternative, and its capture group 4 is `([\\w\\.\\-@]+)` which PERMITS the `@` character; that group is assigned to BOTH `parsed.resource` (line 71) and `parsed.host` (line 72) while `parsed.protocol` is hard-overwritten to the string 'ssh' (line 70) and `parsed.protocols` to ['ssh'] (line 69). So an http(s)-prefixed input that fails parse-path gets relabelled with protocol 'ssh' and a `host` that may embed userinfo \u2014 protocol and host confusion in the returned object, which is the SSRF-relevant decision data consumers act on. The maintainer-documented 'normalize won't work' note means the one sanitizer in the file is known not to defend this path.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from source. All claimed structure is present: the JSDoc gap note at src/index.js:19, GIT_RE at line 38 whose first alternative accepts `http(s)?:\\/\\/` and whose capture group 4 is `([\\w\\.\\-@]+)` (permits `@`), and the fallback at lines 66-79 that assigns G4 to BOTH `parsed.resource` (71) and `parsed.host` (72) while hard-overwriting `parsed.protocol=\"ssh\"` (70), `parsed.protocols=[\"ssh\"]` (69), `parsed.user=\"git\"` (73 - the analyst missed this, it strengthens the claim) and clearing `parsed.parse_failed=false` (75). I verified group numbering by opening-paren order (G4 = the host/userinfo group, G6 = the path group used at line 74), and validated my reading of the greedy G4 against the project's own passing test. Crucially I established the branch is LIVE, not dead code: test/index.mjs:72-86 feeds `git@github.com:IonicaBizau/git-stats.git` and expects host \"github.com\"/protocol \"ssh\"/parse_failed false, output only this fallback can produce since `new URL()` rejects that string. parse-path ^7.0.0 (package.json:45, confirmed via :Dependency) gates parse_failed on WHATWG `new URL()`, and two spec-defined failure modes preserve an http(s) prefix while still matching GIT_RE, so http(s) input genuinely reaches the ssh relabelling. The logic is in the shipped artifact (package.json:5 main -> dist/index.js; dist/index.js:74-87 identical, no added guard), and the graph shows lines 69-75 reachable_from_entry=true at hop_distance=1 from the sole public entry point parseUrl (src/index.js:35). Two corrections to the lead: (a) the JSDoc \"normalize won't work\" note is weaker evidence than framed, since `normalize` DEFAULTS TO false at line 35, so the one sanitizer is absent by default regardless of the SSH caveat; (b) CWE-918 is imprecise, because the branch only fires when WHATWG URL rejects the input, so no fetchable destination diverges from the reported host unless a downstream consumer rebuilds the request from the returned components. That consumer dependence caps severity at MEDIUM; the deterministic, non-consumer-dependent defect is protocol/identity confusion (CWE-436). ADVISORY NOTE: exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so the exploit/version cross-check could not run and I assert no CVE or module identifiers; this verdict rests solely on the source and graph evidence I gathered."
          },
