        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-20",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "The GIT_RE literal at src/index.js:38 captures the \"host\" with the character class ([\\w\\.\\-@]+), which includes the userinfo delimiter '@'. Capture group 4 is then assigned verbatim to BOTH parsed.resource (line 71) and parsed.host (line 72). Because '@' is an allowed host character, a URL such as \"git@github.com@evil.com:path\" has group 4 greedily capture \"github.com@evil.com\", so parsed.host is not a hostname at all but a userinfo+host pair. Under SSH/URL semantics the effective host is the LAST @-segment (evil.com), so a downstream consumer that allowlists on parsed.host with a prefix/startsWith/contains check on \"github.com\" passes the check while the connection actually targets evil.com -- host-spoofing leading to SSRF. The danger is entirely in the literal's character class, not in the match() call. PRECONDITION the verifier must confirm: this branch only executes when parsed.parse_failed is true (line 66), i.e. only for inputs that parse-path itself failed to parse, so the verifier should check whether parse-path fails on a double-'@' git URL.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently and the lead holds. src/index.js:38 defines GIT_RE whose group 4 character class is ([\\w\\.\\-@]+) \u2014 it literally admits '@', the userinfo delimiter, as a host character, while excluding ':' and '/'. For input \"git@github.com@evil.com:IonicaBizau/x.git\" the greediest (and immediately successful, non-backtracking) match gives group2=\"git@\", group4=\"github.com@evil.com\", group5=\":\", group7=\"IonicaBizau/x.git\". That value is then assigned verbatim and unvalidated to BOTH parsed.resource (line 71) and parsed.host (line 72); the only upstream guards are a type/empty check (46-48), a 2048-char length cap (50-52) and optional normalize-url (54-61, default false and documented at line 19 as not applying to SSH urls). The analyst's precondition is CONFIRMED rather than merely assumed: the branch needs parsed.parse_failed===true, and the project's own README:128-141 and test/index.mjs:71-86 show parseUrl(\"git@github.com:IonicaBizau/git-stats.git\") returning protocols:['ssh'], protocol:'ssh', user:'git', pathname:'/IonicaBizau/git-stats.git' \u2014 values hardcoded ONLY inside the if (parsed.parse_failed) block at lines 69-75 (pathname is literally `/${matched[6]}`), with no other code path in src/index.js producing them. So parse-path does fail on scp-style git@host:path and this branch is the normal, documented, test-asserted path for every such URL. Reach is solid: the sole EntryPoint is src/index.js::program:parseUrl at line 35 (kind 'handler', exposure 'reachable') and url is its parameter \u2014 this is the entire public API of a published npm package; the graph marks the lines 66-77 calls reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1. Impact: under both WHATWG URL semantics (last '@' terminates userinfo) and OpenSSH scp-style parsing (strrchr on '@'), the effective host of \"github.com@evil.com\" is evil.com, so resource/host \u2014 documented at README:196 and src/index.js:26 as \"The url domain (including subdomains)\" \u2014 is returned as a userinfo+host pair, and a consumer that prefix/substring-allowlists on it passes while the connection targets the attacker's host; parsed.user is simultaneously hardcoded to \"git\", swallowing the real userinfo. Honest limit on severity: exploitation requires a downstream consumer doing a non-exact match (startsWith/contains) or reassembling a connect string, since a strict equality allowlist would reject \u2014 the standard shape of a library-level host-spoofing defect. Version is pinned exactly to parse-url 8.0.0 (package.json:3), i.e. pre-8.1.0; the exploit catalog was unavailable (\"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"), so no Metasploit module or CVE range is cited and the verdict rests solely on source and graph evidence I gathered here."
          },
